Trace:

  1. _lpTokenValue() reads ICurvePool(LP_TOKEN).balances(0), balances(1), a get_dy(int8,int8,uint256) spot quote, and
  hands them to AbstractLPOracle._calculateLPTokenValue(...), which (per the Notional pattern) computes tokenClaim_i =
  balances[i] * PRECISION / ERC20(LP_TOKEN).totalSupply() and enforces a spot-vs-Chainlink deviation band via the
  lower/upper limit multipliers before summing. The oracle is a view, so no _checkReentrancyContext() write-probe is
  possible — that part of the claim is accurate.
  2. The claim's mechanism is that mid-remove_liquidity, at the raw-ETH raw_call to the attacker, totalSupply is already
  burned while balances[1] is not yet decremented, inflating tokenClaim[1] by 1/(1-f). That ordering is required for
  the claimed inflation. The pool implied by this oracle is a signed-index StableSwap pool (get_dy(int8/int128, ...),
  pool == LP token, balances(uint256) getter) — i.e. the classic ETH-holding pools (stETH/sETH/ankrETH/rETH style).
  Their remove_liquidity is:

  total_supply = self.totalSupply
  for i in range(N_COINS):
      value = self.balances[i] * _amount / total_supply
      self.balances[i] -= value            # coin i decremented
      if i == 0: raw_call(msg.sender, b"", value=value)   # <-- attacker re-enters HERE
      else: ERC20(coins[1]).transfer(...)
  self.totalSupply = total_supply - _amount   # burn happens AFTER the loop
  self.balanceOf[msg.sender] -= _amount

  The LP burn is written after the transfer loop. So in the re-entrant window the oracle observes: balances[0] =
  b0*(1-f), balances[1] = b1 (untouched), totalSupply = S (untouched, not burned). Then tokenClaim[0] = b0(1-f)/S
  (understated by f) and tokenClaim[1] = b1/S (exact). The summed LP value is strictly lower than fair — this is the
  canonical deflationary read-only-reentrancy state (the same one Sturdy-style self-liquidation attacks used), not an
  inflated one. There is no window in this flow where supply is burned while a coin balance is stale; the claimed
  1/(1-f) inflation of the coin-1 leg never materializes.

  3. The other raw-ETH exit points in the same pool family don't produce the claimed state either:
  remove_liquidity_one_coin and remove_liquidity_imbalance both write self.balances and the totalSupply burn before the
  raw_call, so supply and balances are mutually consistent at the callback; exchange doesn't touch totalSupply at all
  and updates balances before the ETH send, leaving only ordinary paid-for spot manipulation, which is precisely what
  the spotPrices[SECONDARY_INDEX] / oracle deviation band in _calculateLPTokenValue is there to bound. The
  burn-before-transfer ordering the claim describes belongs to Curve v2 cryptoswap (get_dy(uint256,uint256,uint256),
  separate CurveToken), which this oracle's signed-index get_dy call and pool-is-LP-token assumption do not address.
  4. Independently, the tie-break bites: AbstractLPOracle._calculateLPTokenValue is not shown, so neither the
  totalSupply divisor nor the quantitative width of the deviation band can be confirmed, and the claim's "high-A keeps
  get_dy inside the band while reserves are several percent off" step is asserted, not demonstrated against any
  configured pool's A and limit multipliers.

  The direction-of-manipulation step that the whole exploit rests on contradicts the actual state ordering of the pool
  type this oracle targets, so the "mint/borrow against an inflated LP price" path does not hold as filed.

  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/oracles/Curve2TokenOracle.sol:_lpTokenValue:73|C2|In the signed-index ETH StableSwap pools this
